<p>The buffer always tracks the student's <em>current</em> zone — it never holds already-mastered questions, and its capacity is finite. When BCQ and NCQ are combined with the buffer, the ablation shows the gains are <strong>super-additive</strong>: replay alone or reformulation alone gives modest gains, but both together compounds far beyond their sum. The buffer keeps re-exposing each hard question to a fresh candidate set on every visit.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §05 -->
<section id="s-scale" data-code="scale">
<div class="section-no">05 — Inverse Scaling</div>
<h2>Widest Zone, Biggest Gain</h2>
<p>ZPPO's advantage is not uniform across model scales — it is largest precisely where the small-student gap is widest. At 0.8B, the zone of proximal development is vast: there are many questions the student can't answer alone but can answer with BCQ scaffolding. At 9B, the student already handles most questions without help.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">scale-chart-canvas — VLM Avg Δ (pp) vs student size</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">hover for values</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="scale-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The pattern is driven by BCQ's structural dependency on teacher success. At 0.8B, the teacher succeeds on many questions where the student fails — a wide pool of BCQ candidates. As the student grows, the remaining hard questions are increasingly ones where the <em>teacher also fails</em>, so BCQ's candidate pool dries up. At 4B and 9B, NCQ's contribution grows relative to BCQ's, since NCQ only needs the student's wrong rollouts, not the teacher's correct ones.</p>
<p>A larger teacher amplifies ZPPO's gain further: expanding to a bigger teacher widens the student-teacher gap and refills the BCQ pool. Shrinking the teacher to 4B or 9B collapses the BCQ signal toward the NCQ-only regime.</p>
<p>The recipe choices also matter at small scales. Iterations per step <em>I=4</em> (vs GRPO's default 16) is the sweet spot: larger I causes gradient drift by running 4× as many updates per step's rollouts; I=1 undertrains at matched step count. Excluding zero-advantage groups from batch normalization ("Norm w/o Zero") avoids inflating the advantage of genuinely-learning groups by including the trivial zero-advantage examples in the denominator.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §06 -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Results</div>
<h2>Distillation Hurts; ZPPO Heals</h2>
<p>The 31-benchmark suite splits cleanly into two groups: <strong>VLM benchmarks</strong> (16 tasks, aligned with the multimodal RL training data) and <strong>LLM + Video benchmarks</strong> (15 tasks, entirely <em>out of distribution</em>). This split exposes the clearest difference between distillation and ZPPO.</p>
<p>Distillation imitates teacher logits. On training-aligned benchmarks it lifts the student marginally — but this comes at the cost of OOD generalization. Off- and On-policy distillation both <em>degrade</em> the same student on LLM and Video benchmarks, because logit imitation concentrates the student on paths the teacher already walked, eroding the student's ability to generalize.</p>

